<p class="lh">Magento catalog search is a conversion driver. When buyers cannot find products quickly, revenue drops—even with strong marketing and competitive pricing. Teams historically chose between Sphinx and Elasticsearch (via Magento OpenSearch/Elasticsearch integrations) depending on catalog size, faceting needs, and operational maturity.</p>

<h2>Sphinx in Magento: strengths and limits</h2>
<p class="lh">Sphinx is lightweight and fast for straightforward full-text scenarios. It can perform well on mid-size catalogs with predictable query patterns. However, Sphinx offers fewer modern analytics features, weaker ecosystem momentum, and limited alignment with cloud-native observability stacks compared to Elasticsearch/OpenSearch.</p>

<h2>Elasticsearch for Magento: why teams migrate</h2>
<p class="lh">Elasticsearch (or managed OpenSearch) provides distributed indexing, richer aggregations, and better tooling for relevance tuning. It scales horizontally for large catalogs, supports complex filters, and integrates with common monitoring stacks. Adobe Commerce cloud offerings also align with Elasticsearch-class search for enterprise merchants.</p>

<ul class="article-detail-list">
    <li>Better faceted navigation for large attribute sets</li>
    <li>Stronger operational visibility (cluster health, slow queries)</li>
    <li>More flexibility for synonyms, boosting, and seasonal campaigns</li>
</ul>

<h2>Decision checklist</h2>
<p class="lh">Choose Elasticsearch/OpenSearch if you have large catalogs, multi-store complexity, or need advanced merchandising rules. Consider Sphinx only for legacy constraints with a clear retirement plan. SanguineIT helps merchants benchmark both paths with real query profiles before migration.</p>

